 selected verification method' qualifier the guidance already uses. -> @owner","F4 [MINOR] G5/B5 (for clarity) -> project-trajectory/prompts/reviewer.template.md, the inserted counterpart/decomposition paragraph -> The file's own dispatcher note says 'DO NOT RE-WRAP THIS FILE. Several phrases are asserted as contiguous substrings by tests/test_agent_loop_review.py; a newline inserted mid-phrase fails them', and the surrounding body is deliberately unwrapped single-line prose. The new paragraph is hard-wrapped mid-phrase ('the same\\nscoped review record must name...', 'purpose and why\\nfurther splitting would add no independent value'), so the file now carries two conflicting conventions and any future test asserting a contiguous substring of this clause - exactly the mechanism SR-185's guidance would be pinned by - fails on a newline the note warns about. -> Concrete change: unwrap the inserted paragraph to match the file's single-line convention, or extend the dispatcher note to state that the counterpart/decomposition clause is intentionally wrapped and must not be substring-asserted. -> @owner"],"basis":"FRESH REVIEW IDENTITY. Attended Critique, human-chosen Opus5/high route; assigned identity docs/reviews/2026-09-06-oi85-record-critique-structured-opus.md, which the launcher binds to a fresh invocation log. Fresh non-author session: this context did not author the prompts, PROCESS_OPTIONS text, skill text, rubric or scoped record under judgment. No provider session id was supplied by the invocation, so none is stated (G1's 'never invented'). Tools were disabled: I judged only the supplied text and claim no code execution, no test run, and no reading of the P9R source or the interfaces/requirements registries beyond the SR/SN rows quoted in the brief.\n\nRUBRIC AND ITS REVISION. docs/rubrics/change-review-records.md, content digest SHA256 81a0de90c98ad2dac0b9774fd94c611181fc49bfde083cc22c52d1fb1ac31f8a (the rubric itself requires a review to name the Git revision or content digest used; the supplied digest is that revision). PARENT INTENT SOURCES, as supplied: stakeholder needs SN-024 (independent critical eye against a written rubric, never the authoring session), SN-037 (boundary-located promises; a reviewed change altering one side of the requirement/interface relationship carries or justifies the counterpart), SN-012 (right-sized process; the proportionality doctrine governs LLR/TC granularity); system requirements SR-184, SR-185, SR-186 (Drafted, Verification=Inspection), with SR-154 and SR-162 read as the adjacent Approved homes. No TC text was supplied and none was assumed; the rubric is derived from SN/SR intent, not from a verifying TC.\n\nRUBRIC-AGAINST-INTENT CHECK (performed before scoring, per the critique brief's inversion rule). G1<-SR-184 identity/rubric/anchor obligations incl. zero-finding coverage; G2<-SR-184's TC-copy failure clause and SN-024's 'not the possibly-lax TC'; G3<-SR-185 and SN-037's final clause, incl. 'resolving two IDs alone cannot establish agreement'; G4<-SR-186 and SN-012, incl. required-tier retention and the no-quota constraint; G5 is an added usability anchor consistent with SR-186's 'delivered process shall require... and record'. The one permissiveness risk I tested: G1 defers unattended family heterogeneity ('unattended family policy remains with the approved routing contract') while SN-024's acceptance requires it, along with bounded iteration and escalation. That deferral is explicit, matches SR-184's own stated boundary, and routes those obligations to SR-154 - Approved, Verification=Test, with logged pre-launch selection and documented degradation - i.e. a stronger enforcer than this rubric, not a silent drop. I therefore applied the rubric as written; it does not buy an approval. I proposed no new B# anchor: F1 sits inside B3, F3 inside B4, F2 and F4 inside B5 ('unusable or contradictory record instructions').\n\nANCHOR-BY-ANCHOR JUDGMENT (all five judged; coverage recorded because some anchors carry no finding). G1 - PARTIAL. The critique prompt's provenance block is materially complete: identify the fresh non-author session, include a provider session id only when supplied, 'never invent hidden metadata', name the rubric path/revision and SN/SR intent sources, record anchors judged including coverage at zero findings; PROCESS_OPTIONS states the same in doctrine and adds the attended variant. The residue is the attended identity instruction being absent from the actionable carrier - carried as F2 rather than duplicated here. G2 - PASS, no finding. The critique prompt orders the check before scoring ('check that the rubric independently covers the supplied SN/SR intent. A rubric copied from a permissive TC without that derivation is a finding; propose the missing numbered anchors instead of approving against it'), which is the operative equivalent of SR-184's 'fails'; PROCESS_OPTIONS states the derivation inversion and the accumulating G#/B# reference; the spine-authoring rule that acceptance states the condition, not the instrument ('a CRITIQUE session returns APPROVE against <rubric>' names a machine), pushes in the same direction. G3 - FAIL, F1. The reviewer prompt itself is correct and followable ('name the changed side and its counterpart and record the independent reviewer's semantic decision... Reference-existence checks support this reading but do not discharge it'), and the H3 paragraph rightly instructs that the reviewer judge the argument against actual source and interface cells; the defect is the unnamed requirement-side counterpart of the IF-011 change. Judged as a stated rationale only, per the brief - the concurrent implementation review owns the cells. G4 - PASS ON SUBSTANCE, one citation gap (F3). The independent-value question, taken as the three SR/direct-TC responsibilities rather than as row counting: SR-184 owns Critique record provenance, SR-185 owns counterpart agreement, SR-186 owns proportionate decomposition; the parents are three different SNs; TC-209 (normal/missing-provenance/TC-copied-rubric), TC-210 (semantic agreement vs valid references) and TC-211 (redundant child while preserving required tiers) each perform verification the parent sentences do not. Collapsing any two would merge distinct failure modes, so I find each child earns its review cost. G5 - FAIL, F2 and F4. Positively: PROCESS_OPTIONS (d2), the spine-authoring stopping-boundary rule, the reviewer prompt's decomposition clause and the planner template's Notes contract ('a row count is not evidence of proportionality') form a followable chain with no new schema, gate, sensor or required LLR layer, and the record's disclaimer is honest about what publishing guidance does not claim.\n\nSCOPE DISCIPLINE. I demanded no new metadata schema, field or automaton; all four concrete changes are edits to existing prose or an added citation, consistent with the Inspection obligations remaining owed. I treat the record's Drafted status and the pending manual Inspection as expected, not as findings: authoring text is not an artifact approval, and nothing here judges the adequacy of the produced implementation. CHANGES_REQUESTED rests on F1 and F2; F3 and F4 are cheap to clear alongside them."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27411,"cache_read_input_tokens":835,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27411},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc1bfc67-23c8-4578-afe9-49380d5f2b04","uuid":"1e997c8f-26bb-467c-a5ad-592e09ead1da","request_id":"req_011CenqBqGpbSK6HQtG6bFYy"}
